# localReceiver Changelog

This file is the changelog and the single source of truth for GitHub
release notes: `.github/workflows/release.yml` parses each `## vX.Y.Z`
section heading into the release title and its body into the release
notes. Batches add bullets under "Unreleased"; cutting a release renames
that section to `## vX.Y.Z — title (YYYY-MM-DD)`.

## Unreleased

## v1.0.0 — renamed to localReceiver (2026-10-05)

- The project is renamed from ttDrop to localReceiver everywhere: app
  and window titles, web pages, the Java package (`localreceiver`), the
  server class (`LocalReceiverServer`), the jar (`dist/localreceiver.jar`,
  release assets `localreceiver_v{x}r{y}n{z}.jar`) and the downloaded CA
  file (`localreceiver-ca.crt`).
- Breaking: settings, paired devices and the CA now live in
  `~/.config/localreceiver/`; environment variables are renamed to
  `LOCALRECEIVER_CONFIG_DIR`, `LOCALRECEIVER_SCHEME`, etc.; the session
  cookie is `localreceiver`; hidden folders are `.localreceiver-part/`
  and `.localreceiver-trash/`. To keep existing pairings and device
  trust, move `~/.config/ttdrop/` to `~/.config/localreceiver/` before
  the first start (otherwise a new CA is generated and devices must
  re-install it and pair again). Leftover `.ttdrop-part/` and
  `.ttdrop-trash/` folders in a shared folder are no longer hidden;
  delete them or rename them to the new names.

## v0.24.0 — hardened TLS and pairing trust model (2026-09-11)

- Security: the CA certificate can now be verified before you trust
  it. The server window prints its SHA-256 fingerprint, and the app
  and the certificate-install page print the fingerprint the device
  actually received (new `/ca-fingerprint` endpoint, plus an
  `X-CA-Fingerprint-SHA256` header on `/ca.crt`) so the two can be
  compared. Installing a certificate authority is permanent, and
  without this an attacker on the network path during first pairing
  could substitute their own CA and go unnoticed.
- Security: the PKCS12 keystores are no longer protected by a fixed
  password compiled into the app. Each installation gets 128 random
  bits, kept in `keystore.pass`; existing installs are re-encrypted on
  the next HTTPS start, keeping their CA so paired devices stay
  trusted. Passwords are passed to keytool through a file, never as a
  command-line argument. The config directory (700) and `ca.p12`,
  `keystore.p12`, `keystore.pass` (600) are now owner-only on
  Linux/macOS.
- Security: clicking the Pair link no longer hands the one-time code
  to the browser on its command line, where any other user of the
  computer could read it — the window shows the code to type in
  instead.
- Security: copying the Pair link now clears the clipboard 60 seconds
  later (only if it still holds that link), limiting what clipboard
  history tools can pick up.
- Fixed: `/ca-fingerprint` no longer shadows same-prefixed webroot
  assets — HTTP contexts match by prefix, so the handler now answers
  only its two exact routes.

## v0.23.0 — device folders decoupled from device names (2026-08-12)

- A device's folder is now independent of its name. Renaming a device
  changes the name only: no files move, the device keeps pointing at
  the folder it already had, and the rename can no longer fail on a
  folder collision (the "dir" error is gone).
- Because of that, several devices can be pointed at one shared
  browsing folder and renaming any of them leaves that folder — and
  the other devices' access to it — untouched. Previously a rename
  moved the folder out from under them whenever the device happened to
  be named after it.
- Pairing still creates a new folder named after the new device, as
  before; the name only seeds that folder, and the host repoints a
  device afterwards with "Folder…" in the server window.
- The server window says so: the Rename… tooltip and dialog note that
  the folder is unaffected, and the per-device path label notes that
  folders are independent of names and may be shared.
- `DevicesTest` covers the new contract: a rename leaves the folder in
  place, and three devices sharing one root all survive a rename of
  the one named after that root.

## v0.22.0 — the file manager release (2026-08-05)

- Uploads now confirm before sending: chosen or dropped files land in
  an upload queue showing name, size, original timestamp, and media
  thumbnails (images and videos), with per-item remove and Clear. A
  drop-down lists the shared folder and its subfolders as the upload
  target; "Upload N files" starts the transfers.
- Uploaded files keep their original modification time instead of the
  assembly time (optional `mtime` on `init`, applied on `complete`).
- Full file manager in the web app: create folder, move (into any
  folder, auto-renaming "name (n)" on conflict), rename, delete — and
  view modes: List, Details (with exact timestamps), Small/Medium/
  Large/Extra-large icons, and Gallery (media thumbnails); the choice
  persists per device. Common video/audio types (mp4, webm, mov, mkv,
  mp3, m4a, wav, ogg, flac) joined the inline-view whitelist so
  thumbnails and direct viewing work.
- Deleting no longer destroys anything: items move to a recycle bin
  (`.localreceiver-trash/`, hidden everywhere) with Restore (original
  location, "name (n)" on conflict) and Remove-forever, per device —
  devices only ever see their own deleted items. New `/api/trash`
  endpoints; client paths can never name the staging or trash areas.
- File management is always on server-side: the global toggle, its
  flags and checkbox are gone — per-device Write and per-subfolder
  deny lists are the only (and sufficient) control. `--fileops`/
  `--no-fileops` are accepted-but-ignored with a notice.
- The shared-folder chooser is bounded to the working directory the
  jar was started from — sharing arbitrary folders above it is no
  longer possible.
- Tests: queue-confirm asserted (nothing uploads before the button),
  mtime preservation asserted, fileops test extended to mkdir/move/
  restore/purge (8 checks); the obsolete fileops-disabled test is
  gone. Full 11-test suite green over HTTP and HTTPS.

## v0.21.0 — shareable links panel (2026-08-05)

- The single URL under the address picker is now a links panel with up
  to three rows, each clickable (opens the default browser), copyable
  (Copy button), and showable as a QR code (QR toggle switches the
  shared QR panel to that link):
  - **App** — `scheme://host:port/`, always shown while running.
  - **Pair** — `scheme://host:port/?pair=CODE`, shown only while
    "Require device pairing" is on. The embedded one-time code is
    minted at start and re-minted automatically whenever a device
    consumes it (or pairing is toggled back on), so the link and its
    QR are always valid. This replaces the "Pair device…" dialog.
  - **Files** — `scheme://host:port/files/`, shown only while
    "Allow directory browsing" is enabled; the row appears and
    disappears live with the toggle.
- Links follow the selected address and the active scheme, as before.

## v0.20.0 — font that renders everywhere, Linux HiDPI auto-scale (2026-08-05)

- Fixed for real: the embedded font loaded (v0.19 proved that on the
  console) but still displayed as a fallback face on Windows 11 and
  Ubuntu — the shipped OTF uses CFF outlines, which some JDK builds
  fail to rasterize even though the family name reads correctly. The
  jar now embeds a TrueType-outline (glyf) static Regular instance of
  Noto Sans TC (instanced from Google Fonts' variable font, SIL OFL),
  which rasterizes on every JDK. The jar SHRANK to ~4.4 MB (glyf
  compresses better). The stale extracted OTF in the config dir is
  cleaned up automatically.
- The L&F test now proves rendering with pixels, not names: text
  drawn with the embedded font must differ from the same text drawn
  with the system Dialog font, for Latin and CJK alike.
- Linux HiDPI: on GNOME desktops with fractional scaling or Large
  Text, Java only honours integer GDK_SCALE and the window rendered
  tiny. localReceiver now reads the GNOME scaling settings at startup and
  sets `sun.java2d.uiScale` automatically (before AWT loads), never
  overriding an explicit `-Dsun.java2d.uiScale=...` or GDK_SCALE.
  Manual override examples are documented in the README.

## v0.19.0 — reliable embedded font on every machine (2026-08-04)

- Fixed: on some machines (observed on Windows) the GUI silently fell
  back to the system font instead of the embedded Noto Sans TC.
  Font.createFont(InputStream) spools through the machine's temp
  directory and can fail quietly; the font is now extracted once to
  `~/.config/localreceiver/NotoSansTC-Regular.otf` and loaded from there,
  with the stream method and finally the system font as fallbacks —
  and every fallback prints a console warning instead of being
  silent. A loaded font is also sanity-checked for CJK coverage.
- Diagnosable theming: startup prints `localReceiver UI font: <family>`, and
  hovering the Stopped/Running status label shows the same — if it
  does not say Noto Sans TC, the console says why.
- L&F test now asserts the embedded family on every component font
  key (Label/Button/CheckBox/TextField/ComboBox/TitledBorder).

## v0.18.0 — named devices and per-subfolder access (2026-08-04)

- Pairing now asks the user to name the device: 1–32 characters of
  lower-case letters, digits and underscore, unique across the
  server's device list (400/409 otherwise, and a rejected name never
  burns the pairing code). Scanning the host's QR code prefills the
  pairing code; the name is always typed by the user. The device's
  folder is created under the chosen name.
- Devices can be renamed from the server window ("Rename…"): the new
  name follows the same rules, and when the device is scoped to its
  own folder that folder is renamed with it — including folders from
  earlier versions whose auto-assigned names carried upper case.
- Per-subfolder access checklist ("Subfolders…" per device): tick or
  untick Read and Write for each top-level subfolder of the device's
  folder. Everything is allowed by default (a deny list), so new
  subfolders are automatically available. Enforced across listings
  (denied subfolders are invisible), direct reads, zips, uploads,
  and rename/delete.
- New app icons in two palettes: the server window/taskbar uses a
  dark-background, light-glyph PNG; the web app uses a light-
  background, navy-glyph SVG + 512px PNG (manifest and
  apple-touch-icon). The NAS glyph (server body, two drive bays,
  status dashes) is an original drawing — the Font Awesome Pro
  reference could not be embedded, its commercial licence being
  incompatible with GPL-3.0 — in the reference's navy #003e6f.
- Test hardening: the browser suite now waits for server readiness
  instead of a fixed sleep (the first HTTPS start generates TLS
  material and could exceed it — the recurring first-test flake).
  New `subdir-acl` browser test (13 checks) and `tests/server/`
  registry test (14 checks: name validation, rename, legacy
  uppercase folders); pairing test extended to 19 checks. Full
  12-test suite green over HTTP and HTTPS.

## v0.17.0 — working folder pickers and versioned release jars (2026-08-04)

- Fixed: choosing a folder was impossible — the folder dialogs
  ("Change…" for the shared folder, per-device "Folder…") rendered
  blank because BasicLookAndFeel ships no usable FileChooserUI. They
  are replaced by a JaCross-drawn folder picker (double-click to
  enter a folder, Up, or type a path); the per-device picker is
  confined to the shared folder, which itself means "everything".
- Release assets are now versioned: the jar on the GitHub releases
  page is named `localreceiver_v{x}r{y}n{z}.jar` for version v{x}.{y}.{z}
  (e.g. `localreceiver_v0r17n0.jar`), so downloads are never confused with
  an older copy. The shared folder remains the directory the jar is
  placed in and run from, served at `/files/`.
- L&F test now renders the folder picker headlessly (24 checks);
  browser suite green.

## v0.16.0 — device pairing and per-device access control (2026-08-04)

- Session per device, on by default: without pairing, a visitor sees
  nothing — every file endpoint returns 401 and the web app shows only
  a pairing screen. Pairing uses a one-time code (10-minute expiry,
  one device per code) shown by the host as a QR code and copyable
  text; scanning the QR opens `/?pair=CODE` and pairs automatically,
  or the code can be typed/pasted into the app. The session lives in
  an HttpOnly cookie; the server stores only its SHA-256.
- Per-device access control, edited live in the new Devices panel of
  the server window: each device has an allowed folder (default: its
  own folder, so devices cannot see the host's files or each other),
  and Read (listings/downloads/zip), Write (uploads, and rename/
  delete together with the global toggle), and Browse (directory
  listing pages, together with the global toggle) switches, plus
  Remove to unpair.
- "Pair device…" dialog in the GUI (QR + copy button); headless mode
  prints a pairing code at startup and a fresh one whenever a code is
  consumed. New flags `--pairing`/`--open`; "Require device pairing"
  checkbox persisted as config `pairing`. `--open` restores the old
  everyone-sees-everything behavior.
- Upload staging is per device, so identical transfer keys from
  different devices cannot collide or interfere.
- The window title now shows the version (localReceiver vX.Y.Z) so it is
  obvious which jar is running; `LOCALRECEIVER_CONFIG_DIR` overrides the
  config directory.
- New `pairing` browser test (15 checks: 401 posture, QR pairing,
  isolation, scoped uploads, code expiry/reissue); the full suite is
  green over HTTP and HTTPS, and the L&F test stays green.

## v0.15.0 — JaCross-themed GUI with embedded Noto Sans TC (2026-08-04)

- The server window is now styled by an embedded Tier 0 subset of the
  JaCross design system (pure java.desktop, new `jacross` package):
  a semantic token layer with OKLab/CIE-L* tonal palettes generated
  from a seed colour, and a BasicLookAndFeel-based `JaCrossLaf` with
  rounded tonal buttons, Path2D check boxes, focus-aware field
  borders, and a flat chevron combo box.
- Design language follows the JaCross default policy: Fluent 2 on
  Windows (small radii, stroked outlines), Material 3 elsewhere
  (pill buttons, tonal surfaces). Dark mode follows the OS
  (reg/defaults/gsettings probes, each optional and bounded, falling
  back to light), and on Windows the OS accent colour seeds the
  palette; the localReceiver brand blue is the fallback seed everywhere.
- The GUI's UI font is an embedded Noto Sans TC Regular (SIL OFL,
  subset OTF, licence shipped alongside): full Traditional Chinese
  plus Latin coverage on every OS with no fallback boxes. This grows
  the jar from ~65 KB to ~5 MB; headless mode loads neither Swing nor
  the font.
- New headless L&F test (`tests/laf/LafTest.java`): ≥4.5:1 contrast
  across all four language×scheme combinations, CJK font coverage,
  and offscreen renders of every themed control. Browser suite
  unaffected and green.

## v0.14.0 — directory browsing, GitHub-style lists, bilingual README (2026-08-04)

- New "Allow directory browsing" toggle (default OFF; GUI checkbox,
  `--browse`/`--no-browse` flags, persisted as `dirBrowse`): when on,
  opening a `/files/` directory URL in a browser shows a server-rendered
  listing page with breadcrumbs and working links; whitelisted types
  linked from it display inline. The PWA's JSON listings are unaffected
  either way (content negotiation via the Accept header), and the pages
  carry a scripts-forbidden CSP with all names escaped.
- File lists restyled after the GitHub repository page — bordered
  rounded list, folders first, folder/file icons, muted size and
  "n minutes ago" columns — both in the app and in the new directory
  listing pages.
- The URL shown in the server window is now a link: clicking it opens
  the page in the default browser (java.awt.Desktop with
  xdg-open/open/rundll32 fallbacks).
- Webroot code style mandate applied: no indentation and no comments in
  served HTML/JS/CSS, lines under 100 characters, UTF-8 declared, and a
  privacy note at the end of each page stating that no external
  libraries, frameworks, fonts, or APIs are used.
- README.md is now bilingual: English (UK) and Traditional Chinese
  (Taiwan glossary), covering the new toggles and styling.
- New `dir-browse` browser test (default-off posture plus enabled-mode
  navigation); full suite green over HTTP and HTTPS.

## v0.13.0 — inline viewing for whitelisted types on /files/ (2026-08-04)

- Opening a `/files/` URL directly in the browser now displays
  whitelisted types inline instead of downloading: common images
  (png/jpg/gif/webp/avif/bmp/ico/svg), pdf, and text-likes
  (txt/md/log/csv/json/xml, all served as plain text). Everything else
  — including HTML, deliberately — remains an attachment download.
- Inline responses carry `Content-Security-Policy: sandbox` and
  `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, so even scriptable formats (SVG)
  cannot run code on the app's origin.
- Browsing inside the app at `/` is unchanged: clicking a file always
  performs the managed (resumable) download, never an inline view.
- New `inline-view` browser test covering both modes.

## v0.12.0 — end-to-end upload integrity digests (2026-08-04)

- The upload worker computes a SHA-256 digest per chunk (crypto.subtle,
  secure contexts) and sends it with the chunk; the server verifies the
  received bytes against it while writing and rejects mismatches with a
  retryable 422, so corrupted chunks are automatically re-sent.
- Digests are optional: uploads without them (insecure LAN contexts)
  are accepted unchanged; malformed digest parameters are rejected.
- Upload test now asserts every chunk request carries a digest in
  secure contexts.

## v0.11.0 — download folders as zip (2026-08-04)

- Every folder in the server list has a ⬇ button that downloads it as
  a zip, streamed on the fly (`GET /api/zip?path=`; empty path zips the
  whole shared folder). Pure `java.util.zip`, nothing staged on disk,
  transfer staging excluded, read-only so independent of the
  file-management toggle.
- New `zip-download` browser test verifying the archive name and
  entries end to end.

## v0.10.0 — per-platform certificate install guide (2026-08-04)

- New `/cert-help.html` page (embedded, offline-capable) with
  step-by-step CA install instructions for iPhone/iPad, Android,
  Windows, macOS, and Linux, plus how to undo and what to do when the
  server certificate is re-issued. The PWA footer's install link now
  leads here instead of directly to the raw certificate.

## v0.9.0 — browser file management off by default (2026-08-04)

- Browser rename/delete is now an explicit opt-in: a new
  "Allow browser file management" toggle in the server window (takes
  effect immediately, persisted) and `--fileops`/`--no-fileops` flags
  for headless mode. Default OFF.
- While disabled, `/api/files/` returns 403 and the PWA hides the
  rename/delete buttons (the listing advertises `fileOps`).
- "Start on launch" remains OFF by default (confirmed and documented).
- New `fileops-disabled` browser test locking in the default posture;
  the suite's shared server now opts in explicitly for the enabled-path
  test.

## v0.8.0 — docs polish and suite hardening (2026-08-04)

- README rewritten around the full feature set: resumable/cancellable
  transfers, folder uploads, browser-side file management, QR sharing,
  shared-folder selection, and the vanilla-only constraint.
- AGENT.md current-state and known-gaps sections synced to reality
  (remaining ideas: CA install instructions per platform, zip folder
  downloads, upload integrity digests, localization).
- QR endpoint test fixed for the HTTPS-by-default era (spawns its
  server with --http explicitly).
- Full verification pass: the six-test browser suite green over both
  HTTP and HTTPS, QR suite 8/8.

## v0.7.0 — choose the shared folder, autostart, --root flag (2026-08-04)

- The server window can change the shared folder ("Change…" next to the
  file root, while stopped); the choice persists in config and is used
  on the next launch.
- File-root precedence: `--root <dir>` flag, then the persisted chooser
  selection, then the working directory the jar was started in. Invalid
  roots are rejected at startup with a clear message.
- "Start on launch" option: when enabled, the server starts
  automatically as soon as the window opens (persisted in config).

## v0.6.0 — manage server files from the browser (2026-08-04)

- Rename (✎) and delete (🗑) buttons on every server-list entry, with
  prompt/confirm dialogs; folders delete recursively after an explicit
  warning.
- New `POST /api/files/rename?path=&to=` (single sanitized name,
  same-directory, 409 on collision) and `POST /api/files/delete?path=`
  (file, or directory recursively). Both resolve strictly inside the
  file root and refuse the root itself and the transfer staging area.
- New `fileops` browser test: rename, delete, and recursive folder
  delete through the real dialogs, verified on disk.

## v0.5.0 — transfer cancellation (2026-08-04)

- Every in-flight upload and download row has a cancel button: the
  transfer worker is terminated, OPFS staging is removed (with brief
  retries while worker locks release), and cancelled transfers no
  longer resume after a reload.
- New `POST /api/upload/abort?key=` endpoint drops the server-side
  staging area for a cancelled upload (idempotent).
- New `cancel` browser test: mid-flight cancel verified to leave no
  client staging, no server staging, no assembled file, and nothing to
  resume.

## v0.4.0 — folder uploads (2026-08-04)

- Upload whole folders: a "Choose a folder…" picker (webkitdirectory)
  and drag-and-drop of directories (entry traversal), with each file's
  relative path preserved — the server recreates the directory
  structure under the file root.
- Upload API accepts a `path` parameter (forward-slash relative path);
  every segment is sanitized like a flat name, traversal is rejected,
  depth is bounded, and collision-safe naming applies within the target
  directory. `complete` returns the relative path.
- Transfer identity now includes the relative path, so same-named files
  in different folders upload side by side.
- New `folder-upload` browser test: three files across three nesting
  levels (one multi-chunk) recreated with hash verification.

## v0.3.0 — HTTPS with a per-user CA, QR sharing, and port fallback (2026-08-04)

- Maintain this CHANGELOG as the source for GitHub release notes.
- README rewritten around the released jar: download from the latest
  GitHub release, headless usage, development section linking AGENT.md,
  CHANGELOG, and the browser test suite.
- GUI: address picker listing the machine's local IPs (NetworkInterface,
  with `ip addr`/`ipconfig`/`ifconfig` parsing as fallback) and a QR
  code of the selected URL so camera devices open the site by scanning.
- New `/qr.png` endpoint: QR PNG of the requesting client's own URL
  (Host-derived), `?text=` override; pure-JDK QR encoder (byte mode,
  versions 1–5, ECC M) verified against an independent decoder — new
  `tests/qr/` suite.
- PWA: lazy "Share this page via QR" section in the footer using
  `/qr.png`.
- Port-conflict handling: when the chosen port is taken by another
  program, the GUI offers an OS-assigned free port and headless mode
  falls back to one automatically.
- HTTPS serving, on by default: the first HTTPS run generates a
  reusable per-user Certificate Authority (`~/.config/localreceiver/ca.p12`,
  exported as PEM `ca.crt`) with the JDK's keytool, then issues the
  server certificate from it (SANs for localhost and the machine's LAN
  IPs, ≤825-day validity). Installing the CA certificate once on a
  device — served at `/ca.crt`, linked from the PWA footer — makes
  every localReceiver session trusted, present and future, unlocking service
  workers and PWA install; deleting `keystore.p12` re-issues the server
  certificate under the same CA. GUI checkbox and `--https`/`--http`
  flags; the choice persists in config. URLs and QR codes follow the
  active scheme. Browser test suite can run over TLS via
  `LOCALRECEIVER_SCHEME=https`.

## v0.2.1 — resumable downloads and config persistence (2026-08-04)

- `/files/` now answers `HEAD` and single-range `Range: bytes=a-b`
  requests (206/416, `Content-Range`, `Accept-Ranges`) with
  `"size-mtime"` ETags.
- New `downloader.js` PWA worker: fetches chunks in parallel via Range
  requests into OPFS staging with per-chunk completion tracking,
  resumes across page reloads, and restarts stale transfers on ETag
  mismatch. Delivery hands the staged file to the browser as a blob-URL
  save; staging cleanup is deferred to the next page load because the
  save streams lazily from OPFS.
- Plain link-navigation fallback where OPFS is unavailable.
- New `localreceiver.Config`: settings persisted at
  `~/.config/localreceiver/config.properties` (last used port, saved when the
  GUI starts the server, loaded as the startup default).

## v0.2.0 — chunked resumable upload engine (2026-08-04)

- New upload API under `/api/upload/`: `init`, `chunk`, `status`,
  `complete`, with metadata via query parameters and raw chunk bodies.
- On-disk staging in `<fileRoot>/.localreceiver-part/<key>/` (hidden from
  listings): atomic chunk writes, exact-size validation, collision-safe
  final assembly, sanitized file names; partial transfers survive
  server restarts.
- New `uploader.js` PWA worker: stages selected files into OPFS via
  sync access handles, uploads missing chunks with a parallel pool
  (default 3 × 4 MiB) and retry/backoff, resumes unfinished transfers
  on page load, falls back to direct `File` slicing without OPFS.
- Upload progress UI in the app; transfers keyed by a stable
  client-side hash of `name|size|lastModified`.

## v0.1.2 — PWA shell (2026-08-04)

- App shell in vanilla JS/CSS: drag-and-drop file picker, server file
  browser with breadcrumbs driven by the `/files/` JSON listings.
- Installable PWA: web manifest, SVG icon, service worker caching the
  shell only (never file data), registered in secure contexts only.
- Light/dark theming via `prefers-color-scheme`.

## v0.1.1 — Java server core (2026-08-04)

- Pure-JDK server (`com.sun.net.httpserver`, virtual-thread executor):
  the working directory is the file root served at `/files/` with JSON
  directory listings, streamed downloads, and path-traversal rejection.
- PWA webroot served on demand from resources embedded in the jar —
  never from disk.
- Swing control window (port, start/stop, localhost and LAN URLs) plus
  `--headless` mode for servers without a display.

## v0.1.0 — pixi environment scaffold (2026-08-04)

- Toolchain managed by pixi: OpenJDK 25 from conda-forge, lockfile
  solved for linux-64, win-64, osx-64, and osx-arm64; `pixi.toml` and
  `pixi.lock` are git-tracked.
- `build`/`run`/`clean` tasks producing `dist/localreceiver.jar` with the
  embedded webroot.
- Initial source layout with placeholder entry point and webroot page.
